WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.020
Hi! Welcome to Biology Professor.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.520
Today we are talking about neurons structure. 

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.740
Neurons are the core cell of the nervous system.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:19.220
They are responsible for transmitting electrical or electrochemical signals to other cells.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:25.680
The most well-known types of neurons are sensory neurons and motor neurons.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.900
These are the cells that when you touch a hot plate, 

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.700
 they transmit the signal to your brain that “hey that HOT!” 

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.280
These are also the neurons that allow you to move your arms around. 

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.900
So let’s look at their structure. 

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.220
Here is an example of a neuron. 

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:43.340
Here we have the nucleus in black and the Soma or cell body

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:48.620
and you can see that branching out of one side of the Soma are multiple Dendrites.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:53.520
These are like fingers. They allow the neuron to reach out into its environment 

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:57.500
and receive signals from other cells.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.240
These signals then propagate down what is called an Axon. 

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.340
If we come over here to look at an Axon in more depth.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.740
You will see that the Axon almost looks like beads on a string. 

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.060
Here the beads are myelin sheets.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.880
So this is when the compound myelin is wrapped around the membrane of the Axon

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.500
and it acts as an insulator. 

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:27.620
it allows the chemical or electrical signal to propagate 

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.030
down the Axon from the node to node.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:36.530
So in between the myelin sheets, we see the nodes of Ranvier.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.430
They are also called unmyelinated gaps. 

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:46.190
and the electrical signal is able to hop, from nod to nod as it goes down the Axon.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.070
Eventually, reaching Synaptic Knobs.  

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:55.100
The Synaptic Knobs are able to able to reach out and connect with other Dendrites.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.780
For example, I have drawn another neuron here. 

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:04.960
These Synaptic Knobs can reach out to Dendrites of multiple surrounding neurons.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:12.080
So that the original signal that this neuron received, gets propagated to other nearby neurons. 

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.600
and that is basically how the nervous system makes things happen in your body.

